WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.330
- It is no cliche

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:04.590
that consultants create
lots of PowerPoint slides.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.960
During my time in consulting at McKinsey,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.810
I probably created several thousand pages

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.410
over the course of six
years that I was there.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.460
But the more experience
you get as a consultant,

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.640
the more you notice that a lot of people

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.940
outside of consulting,

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.040
even those who are working

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.250
for large multinational corporations,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.970
many of them are really not good

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.090
at creating these PowerPoints
slide presentations

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.400
to meet the level and the expectations

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.670
of senior leaderships of large companies.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.340
So we come to another coffee break

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.460
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.690
My name is Heinrich and on
my channel I want to help you

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:44.090
to become successful in the
first years of your career.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.230
And today we are going indeed to talk

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.100
about slide presentations.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.740
By the way, if you wanna know more

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.720
about creating slide presentations

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.400
in the style of consulting firms,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.730
I have a full online course
only dedicated to the topic.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.480
I will link it below in
the video description,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.140
so check it out if you want to
learn more about this topic.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.020
So let's get going.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.970
The insight that I want
to share with you today

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:07.050
is the importance of avoiding clothelines.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.650
And already briefly touched
on this in another video,

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.380
but this concept is so important

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.330
that I really wanted to go
a bit more in depth on it.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.610
So stick in here with me.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.960
I will, of course, add lots
of new thoughts of this

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:18.940
in this video.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.520
So what is a clotheline?

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.860
As a young consultant who
does his or her first slides,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.260
this would be a feedback point

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.910
that you very, very often get,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:31.270
that you really cannot do slides
that contain a clotheline,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.250
but indeed, outside of consulting,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.970
you see slides like that all the time.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.870
And indeed clotheslines are
probably slides like this.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.900
A clotheline is a list of bullet points,

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.250
an unstructured list of bullet points.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.760
Something like this where you
have thought one, thought two,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.430
thought three, four, five, and so on.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.780
So you talk about a
topic, you create a slide,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.870
you write bullet point after bullet point.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.140
That is a clotheline.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.450
But why is this bad?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.010
Where the problem with this is

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.150
that this more or less seems to be

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.840
like a result of your brainstorming.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.980
So you're talking about a specific topic

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.590
and you're just writing
down all your thoughts,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.820
bullet point for bullet point.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.910
But are these not all relevant thoughts?

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.070
Are these all the most important thoughts?

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.260
Are there maybe more thoughts than that?

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.450
Maybe there are, maybe there aren't,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.010
but for the reader of this,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.450
for the recipient of this communication,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.300
it's very difficult to get the confidence

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.510
that the things that you
have here on the slide

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.740
are indeed all the relevant thoughts.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.150
This would be my very
first recommendation.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.410
So what you should do is,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.530
you should always come from a structure,

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.830
say, when you talk about these topics,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.950
there are these two or three main topics

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.140
that you need to consider,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.750
and then in these main topics,

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.220
then there are these thoughts.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.930
So my first recommendation here would be

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.240
that whenever you have a list of bullets

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.070
that you wanna bring
forward for a communication,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.300
you should group them,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.180
you should cluster them into groups.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.830
And then also include
these groups on the slide

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.970
to also communicate the structure.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.690
Because as soon as you do that,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.020
you can say something like,

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.820
so I looked at this topic and
identified three main areas,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.770
three main groups, three
main clusters to look at,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.110
one, two, three.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.130
And then within these clusters,

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.300
then there are the thoughts

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.620
that you wanted to talk
about in the beginning.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.120
Now, in terms of going
even one step further,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.340
what I would recommend you
to do in these situations,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.710
even to format it a little bit more,

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.040
often something like
separator lines like this

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.300
can help to clean it up a little bit more.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.690
But then also let's include a title,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.410
because without that, it
often looks a bit random.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.240
Okay, so there are these
groups, they are these thoughts,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.550
but what exactly are these thoughts about?

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.100
What exactly is this, that
now you are sharing here?

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.080
So often a simple title like that

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.160
can help to create clarity
of your expressions

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.820
of your thoughts that you want to share.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.510
Let's just make an
example that for instance,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.830
you're talk about measures, levers

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.500
that you propose for your business,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.310
for instance, to save costs

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.820
or to grow the business,
or to anything else.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.650
And now let's title it also this way.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.450
Let's title this, for
instance, key measures,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.820
because if you do that, it
also helps your thinking,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.530
it helps your writing that
indeed not every single point

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.210
should be such a key measure,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:12.840
and shouldn't be just
like a random thought

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.370
that you had that you wanted to add here.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.200
Now what I would argue is,
that if it were this way,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.820
it's still not a perfect slide.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.860
It's still a very text-heavy slide.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.560
Now you did introduce some groups,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.150
but it still seems a little bit dull.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.730
And now if you would really
fill all of this with text,

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.320
the slide will likely be very text-heavy

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.810
and then maybe, indeed still
not really making it easy

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.650
to comprehend the key messages

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.420
that you wanna bring forward here.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.430
So what I would recommend
you always to do,

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.370
if you do text-heavy slides like that,

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.470
but think of different categories,

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.950
think in different types of messages

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.010
that you wanna come
across and communicate.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.560
And often for instance, when
you talk about measures,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.330
there will also be other
things that you can talk about.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.260
Maybe for instance, you
would want to introduce

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:54.780
some examples for these measures.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.950
So some very tangible
suggestions of what could happen,

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:59.910
or maybe you have a title of a measure.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.460
and then another information
where you explain this title,

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.200
explain what this is about.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.430
And here this is always
a great opportunity

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.240
now to divide the slide
into different columns.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.920
That you don't only have
one key text column,

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.000
but at least the second one.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.360
Here on this example, first of all,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.110
a description of the measure,

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.350
but then also always an example.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.570
So this often enriches
the slide, adds details,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.940
again, helps you to provide a structure

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.980
and helps it to be more easily digested

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:28.470
because now the person knows

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:29.510
what the measures all about,

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.040
and then also an example

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.980
how to make this more digestible.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.610
So hope you can really see the difference

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:35.840
from going from this,

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.240
so just an unstructured list of thoughts,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.350
to such a structured
version with a header,

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.580
and to maybe even integrating
different types of information

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.130
to enriching the slide in this direction.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.780
I can only tell you
that this is a technique

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.010
that is extremely frequently used

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:53.660
in consulting when I was at McKinsey

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.840
and I trust at all the other
consulting firms as well.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.820
So I can only encourage
you to use it next time

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:58.930
you work on such a slide.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.760
Now I know that I do have many people

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.180
who are also very
experienced in slide writing

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.013
in the audience,

00:06:04.013 --> 00:06:04.846
so let me know, please,

00:06:04.846 --> 00:06:06.980
